            <description><![CDATA[<style>
:root { color-scheme: light dark; }
body { font-family: -apple-system, 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif; font-size: 13px; color: #1a1a1a; margin: 12px; }
@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) { body { color: #e6e6e6; } }
h2 { font-size: 15px; margin: 0 0 8px; } ul { margin: 4px 0 10px; padding-left: 20px; } li { margin-bottom: 3px; }
</style>
<h2>Asobi 0.2 (build 513)</h2>
<ul>
<li><b>DWA-compressed EXRs decode.</b> EXR stills and sequences now decode through OpenEXR itself, so DWAA/DWAB comps — the compression Nuke writes by default — load, play, grab, and optimize like any other render. Previously they refused with "Could not decode", which read as a corrupt file when nothing was wrong with it. Multipart EXRs now load their first part too, and files that already worked are pixel-identical to before.</li>
<li><b>When an EXR truly can't be read, the alert says why.</b> "Deep EXR — not supported" or "DWAA, possibly truncated" instead of a bare shrug — something to tell the vendor.</li>
<li><b>Frame rate and timecode keep coming from the file itself</b> — a DWA delivery's own header numbers show in the HUD and burn-ins, same as every other EXR.</li>
</ul>
]]></description>
