        <item>
            <title>0.2</title>
            <pubDate>Thu, 13 Aug 2026 12:05:33 -0700</pubDate>
            <sparkle:version>507</sparkle:version>
            <sparkle:shortVersionString>0.2</sparkle:shortVersionString>
            <sparkle:minimumSystemVersion>26.0</sparkle:minimumSystemVersion>
            <sparkle:hardwareRequirements>arm64</sparkle:hardwareRequirements>
            <description><![CDATA[<style>
:root { color-scheme: light dark; }
body { font-family: -apple-system, 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif; font-size: 13px; color: #1a1a1a; margin: 12px; }
@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) { body { color: #e6e6e6; } }
h2 { font-size: 15px; margin: 0 0 8px; } ul { margin: 4px 0 10px; padding-left: 20px; } li { margin-bottom: 3px; }
</style>
<h2>Asobi 0.2 (build 507)</h2>
<ul>
<li><b>A package carries the optimized media you already made.</b> Export Session Package has a new <b>Optimized stand-ins</b> setting that copies the stand-ins the session already has and encodes only what's missing — no re-rendering work that is already on disk. Each movie travels with its sidecar, so grades, looks and the display transform still work on the far end, and anyone who has the original footage relinks to it automatically. It's the setting to use when you're sending a review to another Asobi seat.</li>
<li><b>Sequences travel whole, however the session came by them.</b> A conformed cut references one frame of each render, and a package used to collect exactly that — one EXR. The whole render travels now, and the packaged session points at it with a relative path.</li>
<li><b>HEVC and ProRes packages open looking right.</b> Those tiers render sequences with the display transform and the grade baked into the picture, but the packaged session went on describing the original EXR — so opening the package ran both a second time. The package now describes what the file actually is, and the sheet says plainly which settings bake and which stay gradeable.</li>
<li><b>Optimized bundles are complete.</b> Voice-note audio, A/B compare sides, the session gate and the loop scope now travel with a bundle, and a bundle with a QuickTime in it no longer leaves that clip pointing at a path only your machine has.</li>
</ul>
]]></description>
